---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/components.css';
const schema = {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Do underground rooms count against permitted development height limits?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Height is measured above ground level, which is exactly where an earth-sheltered building keeps its profile lowest \u2014 one of its biggest planning advantages."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How long does planning permission take?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A standard householder application runs about 8\u201312 weeks. We build a 12-week window into the project timeline and manage all of it."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What if my application is refused?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Rare on our builds because we pre-check the site position before applying \u2014 that is part of what scoping is for. Where risk exists we tell you before the deposit, not after."}}]};
---
<Layout title="Garden Room Planning Permission: UK Guide | Luxury Modules" description="Do you need planning permission for a garden room? Usually not, and when you do, we handle it. Permitted development rules, size limits and timelines." schema={schema}>
<div class="pagehero">
  <p class="kicker">The Rules, Plainly</p>
  <h1>Garden room planning permission, explained</h1>
  <p>Garden room planning permission, in plain English: you usually don't need it — most garden rooms fall within permitted development. And when you do, we handle the whole thing.</p>
  
  <div style="margin-top:28px"><a class="btn" href="/book-a-call/">Book a Scoping Call</a> <a class="btn ghost" href="#brochure" style="margin-left:8px">Download Brochure</a></div>
</div>
<div class="strip">Official UK Partner of <b>Revonia</b> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <b>1,500+</b> projects across Europe &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <b>25-year</b> structural warranty &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; Planning <b>handled for you</b></div>
<div class="imgph hero"><img class="real" src="/images/modular-40-exterior.jpg" alt="Earth-sheltered garden building with planted roof: minimal visual impact"><span class="lbl">STAND-IN: modular-40-exterior.jpg — shows the planning argument visually</span></div>
<section><div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">The Headline Question</p><h2>Do you need planning permission for a garden room?</h2><div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <p class="lead">Usually, no. Most garden rooms are covered by permitted development rights, which let you build garden structures without a planning application — provided you stay inside the size, height and siting rules below. You will need permission for sleeping accommodation, for commercial use, in conservation areas, and on listed properties.</p>
</div></section>

<section style="background:#efe9db"><div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">The Size Rules</p><h2>What size garden room without planning permission?</h2><div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="spec">
    <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Permitted development limit</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Height (within 2m of a boundary)</td><td>2.5m maximum overall</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Height (elsewhere)</td><td>4m dual-pitched roof; 3m otherwise</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Garden coverage</td><td>Outbuildings may cover no more than 50% of the land around the house</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Position</td><td>Not forward of the principal elevation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Use</td><td>Incidental to the house — offices, gyms and hobby rooms qualify; bedrooms do not</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)">England, general case. Wales, Scotland and NI differ in detail; designated land tightens everything. We confirm your position at scoping.</p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">When You Do</p><h2>When you DO need permission</h2><div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <div class="grid cols3">
    <div class="card"><div class="body"><h3>Annexes</h3><p>Sleeping accommodation means ancillary residential use — a planning application, which we manage. <a href="/spaces/garden-annexe/" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Annexe guide &rarr;</a></p></div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="body"><h3>Commercial use</h3><p>Paying guests change the use class. Standard territory for us. <a href="/commercial/" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Commercial &rarr;</a></p></div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="body"><h3>Protected settings</h3><p>Listed buildings, conservation areas and AONBs need consent — where an underground build is often the most approvable option there is.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="navy-band"><div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">Our Home Ground</p><h2>Underground buildings and planning</h2><div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <p class="lead">Can you build an underground room in your garden in the UK? Yes. Height limits are the binding constraint in most garden applications — and an earth-sheltered building barely has any. Grassed over, it has next to no visual impact, which is precisely what planning committees are asked to protect. It is why our approval record includes settings where a conventional outbuilding would have been refused on sight.</p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">The Offer</p><h2>We handle planning for you</h2><div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:26px">Drawings, applications, submissions and the council conversation are part of every turnkey build — a managed 12-week window inside <a href="/how-it-works/" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">the four-step process</a>. You never fill in a planning form.</p>
</div></section>
<section style="background:#efe9db"><div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">FAQ</p><h2>Planning questions, answered</h2><div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <div class="faq-item"><h3>Do underground rooms count against permitted development height limits?</h3><p>Height is measured above ground level, which is exactly where an earth-sheltered building keeps its profile lowest — one of its biggest planning advantages.</p></div><div class="faq-item"><h3>How long does planning permission take?</h3><p>A standard householder application runs about 8–12 weeks. We build a 12-week window into the project timeline and manage all of it.</p></div><div class="faq-item"><h3>What if my application is refused?</h3><p>Rare on our builds because we pre-check the site position before applying — that is part of what scoping is for. Where risk exists we tell you before the deposit, not after.</p></div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px;font-size:14.5px">More questions? <a href="/faq/" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">See the full FAQ</a>.</p>
</div></section><section class="ctaband" id="cta">
  <h2>Stop reading forums. Ask us.</h2>
  <p>Download the brochure for the full planning section — or book a scoping call and get your site's answer in thirty minutes.</p>
  <div class="btns"><a class="btn" href="#brochure">Download the Brochure</a><a class="btn ghost" href="/book-a-call/">Book a Scoping Call</a></div>
  
  <!-- BUILD NOTE: brochure CTA = HubSpot email-only form (hidden fields: product_interest, product_range, audience_type, source_page_url). Scoping CTA -> contact.html until /book-a-call/ with calendar embed exists. -->
</section>
</Layout>
